Resolve the repository root with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`. Create a precise `ChangeLog/` entry and keep unrelated worktree changes out of the commit.

Audit the intended diff for credentials, personal paths, machine names, hostnames, tailnet identifiers, IP addresses, databases, uploads, environment files, and other private data. Sanitize or exclude every finding before publishing.

Run `npm run verify`. Commit only the intended files, push a new branch to the configured `origin`, and open a new draft pull request targeting `main`. Give the pull request a precise title and description based on the actual change, tests, API/data considerations, and deployment impact.

After the branch is pushed and the draft pull request exists, deploy that exact remote commit immediately by running `npm run prod:deploy:pushed` from the pushed local branch. Do not wait for the pull request to merge.

The pushed-release command must confirm that local `HEAD` exactly matches `origin/<current-branch>`, create an isolated clean worktree at that remote commit, and delegate to the production deploy command. The deploy command owns backup, build, Compose replacement, readiness, release recording, and rollback. Do not run a Docker reset, copy source or live data manually, or run a separate snapshot step. Stop only after providing the pull request URL, verification result, and successful production release result.
